1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,300
Here is a check that should be boring.

2
00:00:03,680 --> 00:00:06,880
The baseline and the candidate are the same class.

3
00:00:07,180 --> 00:00:09,440
Same code, identical inputs.

4
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:11,540
Haruspec doesn't say equivalent.

5
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:13,160
It says unstable.

6
00:00:13,910 --> 00:00:18,730
Look at what the class posts: a total of forty-two, and an order identifier it makes

7
00:00:18,730 --> 00:00:20,470
up on the spot, fresh every run.

8
00:00:21,130 --> 00:00:25,810
The baseline can't even agree with itself, so no comparison against it means anything.

9
00:00:25,810 --> 00:00:28,630
Haruspec says so, and names the field.

10
00:00:30,633 --> 00:00:32,193
That's what this video is about.

11
00:00:32,913 --> 00:00:37,153
Haruspec drives the old code and the new code, and diffs what they did.

12
00:00:37,573 --> 00:00:41,753
A difference only means something if both sides were handed the same world.

13
00:00:42,503 --> 00:00:45,043
So draw a line around the subject.

14
00:00:45,563 --> 00:00:50,423
Everything that crosses it is a seam: collaborators, values, threads.

15
00:00:51,423 --> 00:00:55,163
Haruspec controls each one, so both sides get identical inputs.

16
00:00:55,943 --> 00:01:00,243
When the world leaks in around the line, the two sides see different worlds,

17
00:01:00,243 --> 00:01:03,323
and a difference might be the world, not your change.

18
00:01:05,333 --> 00:01:07,873
The rule that makes this work is plain.

19
00:01:08,193 --> 00:01:11,893
A subject takes what it depends on through its initializer.

20
00:01:12,293 --> 00:01:19,693
Here's a real cart's: its collaborators, a notification center, a queue, even where its order identifiers

21
00:01:19,693 --> 00:01:21,193
and its clock come from.

22
00:01:21,943 --> 00:01:27,183
When a check builds the cart, or your port, it fills every parameter the same way.

23
00:01:27,503 --> 00:01:29,823
First, anything the spec provides.

24
00:01:30,063 --> 00:01:32,863
Then the notification center the capture records.

25
00:01:33,163 --> 00:01:35,723
Then the one stamped double serving that type.

26
00:01:36,063 --> 00:01:41,683
A double records each call made to it, and answers with generated input instead of doing

27
00:01:41,683 --> 00:01:42,243
real work.

28
00:01:42,643 --> 00:01:47,503
If nothing serves a parameter, the check stops and names the declaration to write.

29
00:01:47,763 --> 00:01:48,843
It never guesses.

30
00:01:50,867 --> 00:01:52,467
Values are seams too.

31
00:01:52,867 --> 00:01:53,707
The clock.

32
00:01:53,847 --> 00:01:55,187
A fresh identifier.

33
00:01:55,647 --> 00:02:01,807
Random numbers, user defaults, the keychain, a file, the locale, a network response.

34
00:02:02,227 --> 00:02:04,647
Each is something your code reads from the world.

35
00:02:05,333 --> 00:02:09,993
The fix is the same shape every time: take the source through the initializer,

36
00:02:09,993 --> 00:02:11,933
and let the spec provide it.

37
00:02:12,413 --> 00:02:17,773
This cart's spec hands both sides sequential identifiers and one fixed instant.

38
00:02:17,993 --> 00:02:21,293
Or provide a source that reads through the spec's seams.

39
00:02:21,833 --> 00:02:28,673
Then the value is recorded, replayed, and swept: this planner's clock visits eight instants,

40
00:02:28,673 --> 00:02:32,313
across cutoffs, a Saturday and two holidays.

41
00:02:33,063 --> 00:02:38,583
For a harness you write yourself, Haruspec also ships ready-made recorders for the common ones.

42
00:02:40,500 --> 00:02:42,560
Every seam read lands on a cassette.

43
00:02:42,880 --> 00:02:46,580
The baseline runs first and records each reading, in order.

44
00:02:46,920 --> 00:02:52,520
Then the cassette is replayed into both sides, so the baseline and the candidate are answered

45
00:02:52,520 --> 00:02:53,160
identically.

46
00:02:53,520 --> 00:02:58,640
A double is answered by its arguments, so a port that asks the same questions in

47
00:02:58,640 --> 00:03:00,940
a different order still gets the same answers.

48
00:03:01,690 --> 00:03:04,350
What if the candidate reads past the end of the recording?

49
00:03:05,030 --> 00:03:06,510
Haruspec won't invent a value.

50
00:03:06,730 --> 00:03:10,390
It falls back to the live one, and refuses to call the run equivalent.

51
00:03:10,750 --> 00:03:14,410
The verdict is cannot verify, and it names the seam.

52
00:03:16,433 --> 00:03:18,053
Threads are collaborators too.

53
00:03:18,333 --> 00:03:22,473
A subject that hands work to a queue takes that queue in its initializer.

54
00:03:22,933 --> 00:03:25,513
Production passes a real dispatch queue.

55
00:03:25,513 --> 00:03:29,893
The test target stamps the protocol with the discipline it honors: serial,

56
00:03:30,133 --> 00:03:31,513
concurrent, or main.

57
00:03:32,263 --> 00:03:35,863
Under test, the stamped double holds every piece of work it's handed.

58
00:03:37,463 --> 00:03:41,083
The capture runs it after the operations, one item at a time,

59
00:03:41,283 --> 00:03:43,903
in enqueue order, the same way every run.

60
00:03:44,463 --> 00:03:49,803
Between operations it also explores settle points, where held work lands as if the user paused.

61
00:03:50,983 --> 00:03:55,783
So a port that only breaks after an earlier refresh has landed still gets caught.

62
00:03:56,425 --> 00:04:01,305
Work handed to the main queue runs on the main thread, and main actor code records

63
00:04:01,305 --> 00:04:02,725
as main wherever it runs.

64
00:04:03,185 --> 00:04:08,385
A port that quietly drops its hop to main diverges, and the report names where the

65
00:04:08,385 --> 00:04:08,805
work went.

66
00:04:10,833 --> 00:04:12,973
Notice what Haruspec doesn't do.

67
00:04:13,273 --> 00:04:14,713
It overrides nothing.

68
00:04:14,973 --> 00:04:18,733
It doesn't redefine Grand Central Dispatch or patch your tasks.

69
00:04:19,073 --> 00:04:25,213
So a subject that dispatches directly, spawns a bare task, or calls main actor run is

70
00:04:25,213 --> 00:04:26,173
outside the seams.

71
00:04:26,533 --> 00:04:29,633
The audit reads your source and names it: dispatch.

72
00:04:30,113 --> 00:04:32,553
A green with that finding becomes cannot verify.

73
00:04:33,303 --> 00:04:35,623
The same honesty holds everywhere.

74
00:04:35,983 --> 00:04:39,963
A value the subject makes up reads unstable, and names the field.

75
00:04:40,323 --> 00:04:42,983
A read past the recording reads cannot verify.

76
00:04:42,983 --> 00:04:46,463
Haruspec never silently mocks a seam it doesn't control.

77
00:04:46,743 --> 00:04:49,023
It tells you which one, and what to inject.

78
00:04:51,033 --> 00:04:53,333
Back to the subject that couldn't agree with itself.

79
00:04:53,733 --> 00:05:00,253
Take the identifier's source through the initializer, provide sequential identifiers, and both sides draw the same

80
00:05:00,253 --> 00:05:00,613
ones.

81
00:05:00,913 --> 00:05:04,473
The baseline reproduces itself, and the check reads equivalent.

82
00:05:05,223 --> 00:05:06,043
That's the discipline.

83
00:05:06,823 --> 00:05:11,683
Collaborators, values and threads come in through the initializer, and the spec controls them,

84
00:05:11,903 --> 00:05:13,723
so both sides see one world.

85
00:05:14,103 --> 00:05:16,103
To write the spec, watch specs.

86
00:05:16,583 --> 00:05:19,523
To read every line of a verdict, watch verdicts.
